# Every text file is stored LF, whatever the committing machine's core.autocrlf says.
#
# Why this is a rule and not a preference: git can only normalize a file it believes is TEXT, and
# its heuristic calls a file binary the moment it finds a bare CR. SkirmishBoard.tsx and
# ViewPane.tsx had picked up doubled terminators (CR CR LF) on a handful of lines, which flipped
# both files to `-text` — so autocrlf stopped touching them, their blobs stayed CRLF while all
# 2077 other files were LF, and every edit to either one landed as a whole-file rewrite. A
# two-line change to SkirmishBoard.tsx committed as 5566 changed lines, with the real edit buried
# in 2784 lines of line-ending churn that no reviewer could see past.
#
# `text=auto` closes that off for good: git normalizes on the way IN, so a commit made with
# autocrlf off — another machine, an agent environment, a CI job — can no longer put CRLF in the
# index. The working tree is left alone (no `eol=` here, so checkout follows core.eol/native),
# which is why turning this on changed no file on disk and no blob in the index.
#
# Verify with `git ls-files --eol`: every tracked file should read `i/lf`, except the binary
# pinned below.
* text=auto

# Detected as binary today, declared so it can never be guessed at.
*.wasm binary
